hot-4 — Avoid per-call argument-list consing (plan-3).

Stop allocating a fresh list for every funcall's arguments.

WHAT
- Today a call conses (a1 a2 ... an) and the callee walks it (pop_mandatory_argument
  etc.).  For a variable-heavy load this is a huge chunk of the ~150us and of GC
  pressure.
- Introduce an argument passing convention over a VALUE STACK / reusable Ref buffer:
  the caller pushes evaluated args onto a stack region; the callee reads them by
  index for required params and only materialises a list for &REST.  A required-only
  fast path binds directly from the stack into the (hot-3) frame slots with no
  consing; &optional/&key use a general binder (still off the stack).
- Adapt the callee kinds: tree-walked FUNCTION/CLOSURE bind from the stack;
  BUILT-IN-FUNCTION primitives — provide a stack-based arg accessor alongside the
  current cons-list one (or an adapter) so primitives need not all change at once.
- This is also exactly the VM's calling convention (vm-2/vm-6) — design the stack
  layout so the tree-walker and the VM SHARE it, easing plan-2.
- GC: the arg stack is a rooted scan region (non-moving GC), same discipline as the
  SORT scratch / the VM value stack.

ACCEPTANCE: micro-benchmark call-heavy code showing reduced allocation + time;
&optional/&rest/&key correctness (destructuring, apply) parity; suite green; the
stack layout documented for reuse by the VM.

DEPENDS ON: hot-3 (frame slots to bind into).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NOTE before implementing (from hot-2/hot-3 measurements on this build):
bocl allocation is CHEAP — GC is ~2% on prelude load and ~12% on allocation-
churny loop code, and the per-call arg list is only a fraction of allocations.
The v1.7.2 hot-3 change cut lexical-binding allocations 12% for ~0% wall-clock.
So the "avoid consing" allocation win of hot-4 is expected to be ~TIME-NEUTRAL
here.  hot-4's real TIME payoff is its CPU side: NOT re-walking a consed arg list
in the callee (pop_mandatory_argument) and NOT re-parsing the lambda list on
every call (a cached parameter plan).  Scope hot-4 to those CPU wins (value stack
+ cached lambda plan bound directly into hot-3 cons slots) and MEASURE against
the CPU baseline, not the allocation count — and weigh it against just doing
plan-2 (the byte-code VM), which removes the whole per-form interpretation
overhead that dominates here.
